# Is `parallel for` itself slow, or is the SAFE PATTERN it is forced into
# expensive?
#
# In bench.qu, method [3] is 31x slower than a plain loop. But it cannot use
# the natural shape (accumulate into one vector) because `parallel for` has
# no reduction: it REFUSES a whole-value Vec reassignment with an error.
# This comment previously said such reductions were "silently WRONG", which
# was true when written and stopped being true at a116c451 (2026-08-31);
# re-verified 2026-09-10 by running reduction_check.qu beside this file,
# which now errors rather than printing a wrong vector. The distinction
# matters for anyone reading this to decide whether reductions are safe to
# use: a wrong answer you must detect yourself is a very different hazard
# from a refusal you cannot miss. See bench.qu's header, which was corrected
# first and which this line used to cite for the opposite claim. The
# workaround writes each iteration into its own column of an (N,K) matrix:
# 1,500,000 x 51 doubles = 612 MB, then a sum across it. That is a lot of
# memory traffic the serial version never touches.
#
# So this asks the question the benchmark cannot: given a workload with NO
# big shared buffer -- disjoint scalar writes, real compute per iteration --
# does `parallel for` actually go faster?

n = 48
work = 400000

# --- serial ---------------------------------------------------------------
serial = zeros(n)
tic()
for k = 0 to n - 1
    acc = 0.0
    for j = 1 to work
        acc = acc + sin(j * 0.000001 + k)
    end for
    serial[k] = acc
end for
t_serial = toc()

# --- parallel, disjoint scalar writes (the pattern the engine supports) ---
par = zeros(n)
tic()
parallel for k in 0 to n - 1
    pacc = 0.0
    for j = 1 to work
        pacc = pacc + sin(j * 0.000001 + k)
    end for
    par[k] = pacc
end parallel
t_par = toc()

d = max(abs(serial - par))
print("serial            : {t_serial:.4f} s")
print("parallel for      : {t_par:.4f} s")
print("speedup           : {t_serial / t_par:.2f}x")
print("max|serial - par| : {d:.3e}   (0 = same answer)")
